{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 24, :comments [{:quotes [{:he "אנכי אלהי אברהם אביך", :translit "anokhi elohei avraham avikha", :gloss "I am the Elohim of Abraham your father"} {:he "ה' אלהים", :translit "YHWH Elohim", :gloss "YHWH Elohim"}], :mechanisms [{:kind :other, :note "Contrasts the divine name used here vs. with Abraham and Jacob"}], :frame {:text "The verse addressing Isaac reads 'Elohim' alone (Genesis 26:24), whereas Abraham is addressed with the tetragrammaton (Genesis 28:13 addresses Jacob with YHWH). The name Elohim connotes strict justice, while the tetragrammaton connotes mercy, as established in the midrashic reading of Exodus 34:6. The Talmud states that the righteous accept suffering with love, and Isaac's sufferings are discussed in rabbinic literature (Bava Batra 16b discusses whether Isaac's blindness was a punishment).", :sources ["Genesis 26:24" "Genesis 28:13" "Exodus 34:6" "Bava Batra 16b"]}}], :arithmetic [{:kind :other, :note "Contrasts the divine name used here vs. with Abraham and Jacob", :by :selah, :ran? false}], :book :genesis, :voice :baal-haturim, :lang :en, :chapter 26, :discipline "bht-chalkboard-v1", :timestamp "2026-07-12T23:08:23.563468387Z", :model "glm-5.2"}